// The systems' classification (ADR-0013 §2; read by rv/world/building.hpp, which documents the format): every system of
// ES-DE 3.4.1's es_systems.xml (tests/fixtures/world/esde-3.4.1-systems.txt lists their names; a unit test keeps the
// table total over it), each with its kind (home, handheld, arcade, computer, other or excluded), the generation of a
// home console or a handheld (1 to 9, Wikipedia's generations of video game consoles by first release), its media
// family (media_families.json), its entry order on its floor, a generic sign and its ES-DE platform tags
// (es_systems.xml's <platform>, which the fallback compares). Signs are generic texts, never a trade name (style §1,
// §17.2); full names never enter this file.
//
// The rows are in the floors' order (building.json): the arcade systems, the home consoles by generation, the
// handhelds, the computers, the kind "other", the excluded systems. The order numbers sort the systems that enter a
// floor at once: a band per floor (1xx arcade, 2xx generations 1 and 2, 3xx to 6xx generations 3 to 6, 65x to 69x
// generations 7 to 9, 7xx handhelds, 8xx computers, 9xx other), in it the principal systems of the generation first
// (I4 step 1's rows kept as they were: the plan goldens hash snes's and megadrive's), then their regional variants and
// add-ons by maker. A system the table does not name takes the entry of the first system of the table (in the file's
// order) that shares one of its platform tags: "arcade" for an arcade tag, "pc" for "pc", the first computer
// ("windows9x") for "pcwindows"; failing that, it is "other" with the fallback's family, order and sign. Excluded
// systems get no room: their games stay in the quick catalog.
//
// Families (ADR-0013 §4; the rare formats take the nearest): home cartridges by generation (8-bit for generations 2
// and 3, 16-bit for 4, 64-bit for 5 and later; the disk cards of the fds take the handheld cartridge), CD jewel cases
// for the CD systems and the HuCard consoles (whose cards came in jewel cases), DVD keep cases from the sixth
// generation, handheld cartridges and cases, big boxes for the 16- and 32-bit computers and the PCs, the generic case
// for everything else (the arcade systems, the 8-bit computers, the LCD games, the kind "other"). Since I6 step 4
// (ADR-0014 §2) the cases and boxes are the *_v2 families, standing on their side with an upright spine; a room planned
// with their predecessors is migrated once by the update.
//
// The defaults per kind give a room's theme pack, its console prefab (none for arcade, computer and other: the hero
// corner stands without one) and its plastics (sRGB hex codes); assets/world/platforms/<system>.json, when it exists,
// overrides them for its system (snes.json). A floor's shared room and the arcade room (ADR-0013 §6) take the defaults
// of their floor's first kind without a console, and that kind's "sign" (the arcade room's "ARCADE"), else the
// fallback's.
